ng a common whole", "required": true, "content": {"body": "Compare fractions using the same whole. With a common denominator, the fraction with the larger numerator represents more equal-sized parts.", "competencyKeys": ["foundation-3"], "prerequisites": [], "modes": [{"label": "Focus on the key idea", "text": "Compare fractions using a common whole. With a common denominator, the fraction with the larger numerator represents more equal-sized parts."}], "quickCheck": {"prompt": "For the same whole, which is larger?", "options": ["Three fifths", "Two fifths"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Three fifths. Compare fractions using the same whole. With a common denominator, the fraction with the larger numerator represents more equal-sized parts."}}}, {"title": "Combine fractions", "block_type": "relationships", "sequence": 3, "objective": "Add and subtract fractions with compatible denominators", "required": true, "content": {"body": "To add fractions, first express them in equal-sized parts. One quarter plus two quarters is three quarters; keep the denominator because the parts remain quarters.", "competencyKeys": ["foundation-4"], "prerequisites": [], "modes": [{"label": "Focus on the key idea", "text": "Add and subtract fractions with compatible denominators. One quarter plus two quarters is three quarters; keep the denominator because the parts remain quarters."}], "quickCheck": {"prompt": "What is one quarter plus two quarters?", "options": ["Three eighths", "Three quarters"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Three quarters. To add fractions, first express them in equal-sized parts. One quarter plus two quarters is three quarters; keep the denominator because the parts remain quarters."}}}, {"title": "Unequal parts", "block_type": "misconception", "sequence": 4, "objective": "Correct unequal-part and denominator misconceptions", "required": true, "content": {"body": "A shape divided into four pieces does not automatically show quarters. Each piece must have one quarter of the whole's area for each piece to represent a quarter.", "competencyKeys": ["foundation-5"], "prerequisites": [], "modes": [{"label": "Focus on the key idea", "text": "Correct unequal-part and denominator misconceptions. Each piece must have one quarter of the whole's area for each piece to represent a quarter."}], "quickCheck": {"prompt": "Four unequal pieces are cut from a cake. Is each piece a quarter?", "options": ["No, quarters must be equal portions", "Yes, there are four pieces"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "No, quarters must be equal portions. A shape divided into four pieces does not automatically show quarters. Each piece must have one quarter of the whole's area for each piece to represent a quarter."}}}, {"title": "Sharing a quantity", "block_type": "application", "sequence": 5, "objective": "Use a fraction to describe a shared quantity", "required": true, "content": {"body": "Sharing eight oranges equally between four learners gives two oranges per learner. Each learner receives one quarter of the total, so one quarter of eight is two.", "competencyKeys": ["foundation-6"], "prerequisites": [], "modes": [{"label": "Focus on the key idea", "text": "Use a fraction to describe a shared quantity. Each learner receives one quarter of the total, so one quarter of eight is two."}], "quickCheck": {"prompt": "Eight oranges are shared equally among four learners. How many does each receive?", "options": ["Four", "Two"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Two. Sharing eight oranges equally between four learners gives two oranges per learner. Each learner receives one quarter of the total, so one quarter of eight is two."}}}]}, {"slug": "ratios-proportion", "concept_type": "quantitative", "competencies": [{"depth": "foundation", "key": "foundation-1", "description": "Interpret a ratio as a comparison of quantities", "evidence_type": "quick_check", "sequence": 0}, {"depth": "foundation", "key": "foundation-2", "description": "Identify the order 